#!/usr/sbin/nft -f

# Scope the wired management bridge (br-mgmt) to the administration surfaces.
#
# This include is loaded inside fw4's `inet` table (same shape as
# 20-nds-enforce.nft, 30-backend-firewall.nft, 31-admin-board-not-guest-reachable.nft
# and 32-luci-not-guest-reachable.nft), so it runs on `fw4 reload` and survives a
# nodogsplash restart.
#
# WHAT br-mgmt IS. The wired LAN ports are moved off the captive bridge onto a
# bridge of their own by `setup_mgmt_bridge` in 99-tollgate-setup, so the port an
# operator administers from stops sharing a layer-2 domain with the strangers on
# the open guest SSID, and so the administration surfaces answer his cable
# before he pays anything. The decision, the evidence and the rejected
# alternatives are docs/architecture/lan-port-management-bridge-decision.md
# (PR #599). This fragment is its D4/D5 half: the bridge is an ALLOW LIST of
# administration surfaces and nothing else.
#
# WHY AN ALLOW LIST AND NOT A BLACKLIST. Because it has to carry a property a
# blacklist cannot state: br-mgmt cannot transact. One nodogsplash process gates
# exactly one bridge (a single `gw_interface`, fixed shared `nds*` chain names,
# one control socket), so the wired bridge has NO captive gate. A client on a
# bridge this module cannot gate must not be able to buy: it resolves a MAC
# through the dnsmasq lease it gets from `dhcp.mgmt`, can be quoted, can pay, and
# the grant then fails in the single instance's `ndsctl` answer
# `Client <mac> not found.` — money taken, nothing delivered. So the payment
# surfaces (`:2050`, `:2051`, `:2121`) are deliberately NOT in the allow list,
# and the catch-all drop below is what makes that structural rather than
# incidental: `:2121` is a bridge that sells. Reopening any of the three belongs
# to the F1/F2 follow-up in the ADR (a real second gate), not to a wider list.
#
# WHY THE ADMIN PORTS ARE REACHABLE HERE. `:443`/`:8080` (LuCI, uhttpd.main) and
# `:8090`/`:8443` (the TollGate board, uhttpd.admin) are dropped on the CAPTIVE
# bridge by 32-luci-not-guest-reachable.nft and
# 31-admin-board-not-guest-reachable.nft, both of which match `iifname "br-lan"`.
# Those guards are about a stranger on an open SSID — the board is a
# root-capable login (`file:["exec"]`, `system:["password_set"]`,
# `tollgate wallet_drain_cashu` behind rpcd's session) and :8090 serves it over
# cleartext HTTP. br-mgmt holds no stranger: reaching it means walking to the
# router and plugging in a cable, which is the same trust boundary as the
# operator's own hands. That is why the four ports are reachable here and stay
# dropped there, and why this fragment must never be extended to `br-lan` and the
# two guards must never be extended to `br-mgmt` (both directions are pinned by
# tests/packaging/mgmt-bridge-scope_test.sh).
#
# WHAT IS DROPPED, INCLUDING BY THE CATCH-ALL: everything that is not DHCP, DNS,
# SSH or one of the four administration ports. No forwarding to the WAN exists
# for this bridge either (`firewall.mgmt_zone` has `forward 'REJECT'` and no
# `config forwarding`), so the cable has no internet at all — that is the
# deliberate consequence of "one gate" that makes the payment-surface half
# honest, not an oversight. ICMP is dropped too: a ping to the router's own
# address fails by design here; `:22` is the diagnostic path.
#
# CAVEAT, same as 30-backend-firewall.nft:15-17 — this assumes the wired
# management bridge is named br-mgmt, which is what `setup_mgmt_bridge` writes.
# An operator who renamed it loses this rule and nothing else (the ports then
# fall back to the zone's input policy).

chain mgmt_bridge_scope {
	type filter hook input priority -1; policy accept

	# What a client needs to exist on this bridge at all: a lease and DNS.
	meta nfproto ipv4 iifname "br-mgmt" udp dport { 53, 67, 68 } counter accept
	meta nfproto ipv4 iifname "br-mgmt" tcp dport 53 counter accept

	# SSH: one of the two documented recovery paths (the other is the private
	# SSID), and the only diagnostic that works here because ICMP is dropped.
	meta nfproto ipv4 iifname "br-mgmt" tcp dport 22 counter accept

	# The four administration surfaces: LuCI (:8080, and the :443 TLS listener
	# uhttpd.main opens when a cert/key exists) and the board (:8090, and its
	# opt-in :8443 TLS listener). See the header for why they are reachable
	# here and nowhere else.
	meta nfproto ipv4 iifname "br-mgmt" tcp dport { 443, 8080, 8090, 8443 } counter accept

	# No IPv6 is configured on this bridge (`network.mgmt.ip6assign='0'`,
	# `dhcp.mgmt.ra`/`dhcpv6` disabled — the same posture the captive bridge
	# takes in setup_disable_ipv6_lan), so nothing legitimate arrives over IPv6
	# and the family is dropped outright rather than left to the zone policy.
	meta nfproto ipv6 iifname "br-mgmt" counter drop

	# The tail: everything else from the wired bridge, including the payment
	# surfaces (`:2050`, `:2051`, `:2121`) and any path off the router that is
	# not one of the accepts above. Keep it last.
	meta nfproto ipv4 iifname "br-mgmt" counter drop
}
